## TL;DR  
1. **Add the `pmtiles` JS library** (via `<script>` tag or npm).  
2. **Register a protocol handler** (`pmtiles://`) that tells MapLibre how to read the archive.  
3. **Create a source that points to the archive** (`type: "vector"` / `"raster"` / `"geojson"` as appropriate).  
4. **Add a layer that uses that source** (or reference the source in a style JSON).  

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready example** that shows a vector‑tile PMTiles archive (`my‑tiles.pmtiles`) being used as a source in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1. Why a special library is needed

`*.pmtiles` is a **single‑file container** for tiled raster or vector data (MVT, PNG, JPEG, etc.).  
MapLibre GL JS can only read tiles over HTTP/HTTPS, so we need a **protocol handler** that:

* Opens the file (or remote URL) as a binary blob.  
* Serves individual tiles on demand (e.g., `GET /{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`).  

The official library that does this is **`pmtiles`** (formerly `@mapbox/pmtiles`). It registers a custom URL scheme (`pmtiles://`) that MapLibre treats just like any other tile URL.

---

## 2. Install / load the library

### Option A – CDN (quick demo)

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@2.5.0/dist/pmtiles.min.js"></script>
```

### Option B – npm (production)

```bash
npm install pmtiles maplibre-gl
```

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles, register } from 'pmtiles';
```

> **Tip:** The CDN version bundles the library in a global `pmtiles` object, while the npm version exports named symbols. The usage is identical; just adjust the import style.

---

## 3. Register the protocol handler

You must **register a `PMTiles` instance** *before* you add any source that uses the `pmtiles://` scheme.

```js
// 1️⃣ Create a PMTiles object that points to your archive.
//    It can be a remote URL (CORS‑enabled) or a relative path on the same origin.
const pm = new pmtiles.PMTiles('https://example.com/tiles/my-tiles.pmtiles');

// 2️⃣ Register it with the global protocol registry.
pmtiles.register(pm);
```

If you have **multiple archives** you can register each one separately:

```js
const pmA = new pmtiles.PMTiles('tiles/a.pmtiles');
const pmB = new pmtiles.PMTiles('tiles/b.pmtiles');
pmtiles.register(pmA);
pmtiles.register(pmB);
```

The library will automatically route requests like `pmtiles://my-tiles.pmtiles/12/3456/7890.pbf` to the correct archive.

---

## 4. Add the source to the map

### 4.1 Vector tiles (most common)

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The URL is the *archive name* (the string you passed to PMTiles) prefixed with the protocol.
  url: 'pmtiles://my-tiles.pmtiles'   // <-- note: no /{z}/{x}/{y} part!
});
```

### 4.2 Raster tiles (PNG/JPEG)

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  url: 'pmtiles://my-raster.pmtiles',
  tileSize: 256   // or 512, depending on how the archive was created
});
```

### 4.3 GeoJSON (if the archive contains a single GeoJSON file)

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-geojson', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'pmtiles://my-geojson.pmtiles'   // works because the library can return the whole blob
});
```

> **Important:** The `url` **must be exactly the same string you used when constructing the `PMTiles` object** (case‑sensitive). The library uses that string as a key to look up the right archive.

---

## 5. Add a layer that consumes the source

#### Vector‑tile example (MVT)

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pmtiles-layer',
  type: 'fill',               // or 'line', 'symbol', etc.
  source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
  // The name of the layer inside the PMTiles archive (usually the same as the source layer in the original MBTiles)
  'source-layer': 'roads',
  paint: {
    'fill-color': '#ff6600',
    'fill-opacity': 0.5
  }
});
```

#### Raster‑tile example

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pmtiles-raster-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'my-pmtiles-raster',
  paint: {
    'raster-opacity': 0.9
  }
});
```

---

## 6. Full working HTML page (CDN version)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles Demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <!-- PMTiles library -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@2.5.0/dist/pmtiles.min.js"></script>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Register the PMTiles archive
  const pm = new pmtiles.PMTiles('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visgl/pmtiles/master/examples/nyc/nyc.pmtiles');
  pmtiles.register(pm);

  // 2️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add layers later
    },
    center: [-73.985130, 40.758896], // Times Square
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 3️⃣ When the map is ready, add the source + layer
  map.on('load', () => {
    // Vector source from the PMTiles archive
    map.addSource('nyc-pmtiles', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'pmtiles://nyc.pmtiles'   // <-- matches the name we gave to PMTiles()
    });

    // Add a layer that draws the "building" source-layer from the archive
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'nyc-buildings',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'nyc-pmtiles',
      'source-layer': 'building',   // <-- check the archive's layer names (use pmtiles-cli or MBUtil to inspect)
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#6a5acd',
        'fill-opacity': 0.6
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What this page does**

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **PMTiles registration** | Opens the remote `.pmtiles` file, reads its header, and makes the archive addressable via `pmtiles://nyc.pmtiles`. |
| **MapLibre init** | Starts a blank style (no sources). |
| **`addSource`** | Tells MapLibre that the source is a vector source whose tiles will be fetched from the custom protocol. |
| **`addLayer`** | Renders the `building` layer that lives inside the archive. |

You can replace the URL with **any** `.pmtiles` file you own (just make sure CORS is allowed or serve it from the same origin).  

