Below is a **step‑by‑step guide** that shows you how to load a **PMTiles raster‑DEM (elevation) file** and turn it into a 3‑D terrain layer in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1. What you need beforehand

| Item | Why you need it | How to get it |
|------|----------------|---------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** (≥ v2.4) | Core map library that supports the `terrain` API. | `<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>` |
| **PMTiles JavaScript library** | Lets MapLibre read `pmtiles://…` URLs. | `<script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@latest/dist/pmtiles.js"></script>` |
| **A raster‑DEM PMTiles file** (e.g. `elevation.pmtiles`) | The actual elevation data. | Create it yourself (see §2) or download a ready‑made one (e.g. from AWS Open Data). |
| **A style that includes a base raster or vector layer** | Terrain works on top of any map style. | Use the default MapLibre style, a Mapbox style, or your own JSON. |

> **Tip:** If you are serving the PMTiles file from the same origin as your page you can just reference it directly. If you host it on a different domain, make sure the server sends `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your domain) – otherwise the browser will block the tiles.

---

## 2. (Optional) Create a PMTiles DEM yourself

If you already have a GeoTIFF DEM (e.g. SRTM, Copernicus DEM, USGS NED) you can turn it into a PMTiles archive with the **official `pmtiles` CLI**:

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install the CLI (requires Node ≥ 18)
npm install -g @mapbox/pmtiles

# 2️⃣ Convert the GeoTIFF into a tiled raster (256 px tiles, EPSG:3857)
#    gdal2tiles.py is part of GDAL; you can also use `gdal_translate -of XYZ`
gdal2tiles.py -z 0-14 -a 0 -s EPSG:4326 my_dem.tif ./tiles

# 3️⃣ Pack the tiles into a PMTiles container
pmtiles create ./tiles elevation.pmtiles
```

*If you already have a tiled DEM (e.g. from a cloud‑optimized GeoTIFF or a pre‑tiled MBTiles), just run `pmtiles create` on that folder.*

**Result:** `elevation.pmtiles` – a single file that can be served via HTTP, S3, Cloudflare R2, etc.

---

## 3. Add the PMTiles protocol to MapLibre

The PMTiles library registers a custom URL protocol (`pmtiles://`). You only have to do this **once** before you create the map.

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@latest/dist/pmtiles.js"></script>
<script>
  // Register the protocol globally
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles.Protocol();
  pmtiles.registerFetch();
</script>
```

> `registerFetch()` patches the global `fetch` function so that any request whose URL starts with `pmtiles://` is intercepted and served from the PMTiles archive.

---

## 4. Build the MapLibre map and add the terrain source

Below is a **complete minimal HTML page** that demonstrates everything. Replace `YOUR_STYLE_URL` with the style you want (or keep the default MapLibre style) and replace `elevation.pmtiles` with the path to your PMTiles file.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles Terrain</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <!-- PMTiles library -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@latest/dist/pmtiles.js"></script>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Register the PMTiles protocol (must happen before map creation)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles.Protocol();
  pmtiles.registerFetch();   // patches global fetch

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,          // tilt the camera so you can see the 3‑D effect
    bearing: -20,
    antialias: true     // smoother terrain rendering
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Add the DEM source (type: raster-dem)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      // The URL must start with the custom protocol we registered:
      url: 'pmtiles://elevation.pmtiles',
      // Optional but recommended:
      tileSize: 256,          // same as the tiles you generated
      maxzoom: 14,           // highest zoom level you have in the PMTiles
      // If your DEM is encoded as 16‑bit unsigned (most DEMs are):
      encoding: 'mapbox'    // tells MapLibre to interpret the data as elevation in meters
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Enable terrain on the map
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      exaggeration: 1.0   // >1 makes mountains look taller, <1 flattens them
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ (Optional) Add a sky layer for a nicer look
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sky',
      type: 'sky',
      paint: {
        'sky-type': 'atmosphere',
        'sky-atmosphere-sun': [0.0, 0.0],
        'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Code snippet | Purpose |
|--------------|---------|
| `pmtiles.registerFetch()` | Makes `fetch('pmtiles://…')` read from the local PMTiles file instead of trying a network request. |
| `type: 'raster-dem'` | Informs MapLibre that the source contains elevation data (not imagery). |
| `url: 'pmtiles://elevation.pmtiles'` | The custom protocol points to the PMTiles archive you uploaded to the same server (or a CDN). |
| `tileSize: 256` | Must match the tile size you used when creating the PMTiles. |
| `encoding: 'mapbox'` | Most DEM tiles are stored as unsigned 16‑bit values where the value = elevation in meters. This flag tells MapLibre to decode them correctly. |
| `map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1})` | Turns the DEM into a 3‑D terrain surface. |
| `sky` layer (optional) | Gives a realistic horizon and atmospheric shading. |

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No terrain appears (flat map)** | The DEM source never loads or is not recognized. | Open the browser dev‑tools → Network → look for a request to `pmtiles://…`. If you see a 404, the URL is wrong. If you see a CORS error, add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` on the server. |
| **Terrain looks “noisy” or “pixelated”** | Tiles are at a lower zoom than the map view. | Make sure `maxzoom` in the source matches the highest zoom you generated. If you only have up to zoom 12, don’t zoom the map beyond that (or generate more zoom levels). |
| **Elevation values are off by a factor of 1000** | The DEM was encoded as **float32** instead of **uint16**. | Use `encoding: 'mapbox'` for uint16 (default). If you have a float DEM, omit the `encoding` property and set `encoding: 'terrain'` (MapLibre will treat the values as meters directly). |
| **Map freezes while loading terrain** | Large PMTiles file (hundreds of MB) being fetched all at once. | Serve the file with **byte‑range** support (most HTTP servers do this automatically). The PMTiles library will request only the needed tiles. |
| **Terrain works locally but not on production** | The production server does not support **Range** requests. | Verify that `Accept-Ranges: bytes` is present in the response headers. If not, enable it (e.g., `nginx: add_header Accept-Ranges bytes;`). |
| **Exaggeration looks too strong** | `exaggeration` set > 1. | Reduce the value (e.g., `0.5` for half‑height). |

---

## 6. Going further – Adding a hillshade or contour layer

If you want to **visualize the terrain** beyond the 3‑D surface, you can add a hillshade raster layer that reads from the same DEM source:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5
  }
});
```

Or draw **contour lines** using a vector source generated from the DEM (e.g., with `gdal_contour` → MBTiles → PMTiles). The same `pmtiles://` protocol works for vector tiles as well.

---

## 7. Full production checklist

| Item | Checklist |
|------|-----------|
| **MapLibre version** | ≥ 2.4 (terrain API introduced in 2.4). |
| **PMTiles file** | Served with `Content-Type: application/octet-stream` and `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. |
| **CORS** | If cross‑origin, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **HTTPS** | Browsers block mixed‑content; serve both map page and PMTiles over HTTPS. |
| **Cache** | Set `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000` for the PMTiles file to avoid re‑downloading on each visit. |
| **Performance** | Keep tile size 256 px, maxzoom ≤ 14 for most global DEMs (higher zooms increase file size dramatically). |
| **Testing** | Verify on Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge. Mobile browsers need `antialias: true` for smoother terrain. |
| **Fallback** | Provide a non‑terrain style for browsers that don’t support WebGL2 (MapLibre will automatically fall back). |

---

## 8. TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

```js
// 1️⃣ Register PMTiles protocol
const pm = new PMTiles.Protocol();
pm.registerFetch();

// 2️⃣ Create map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
  pitch: 60,
  antialias: true
});

// 3️⃣ Add DEM source from PMTiles
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addSource('terrain', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'pmtiles://elevation.pmtiles',
    tileSize: 256,
    maxzoom: 14,
    encoding: 'mapbox'
  });

  // 4️⃣ Enable terrain
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1 });

  // 5️⃣ Optional sky layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'sky',
    type: 'sky',
    paint: { 'sky-type': 'atmosphere' }
  });
});
```

That’s it! You now have a fully 3‑D terrain map powered by a **PMTiles** elevation archive. Feel free to experiment with different DEM sources, exaggeration values, and additional visual layers (hillshade, contours, satellite imagery) to get the exact look you need. Happy mapping!
